    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          No, induction cooking does not use more electricity; it typically uses less per meal than a radiant electric cooktop, because it puts roughly 85 to 90 percent of its energy into the pan versus about 70 to 80 percent for radiant elements. The big wattage figures on induction cooktops describe peak power draw, not what you use, and a typical meal costs only a few cents to cook. The real electrical question is the circuit: a full-size cooktop usually needs a dedicated 240 volt circuit, commonly 40 to 50 amps, while portable burners run on a standard 120 volt outlet at up to 1,800 watts.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Does an Induction Cooktop Run Up Your Electric Bill?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not compared with other electric cooktops. Induction heats the pan directly with a magnetic field, so less heat is lost into the glass and the air. ENERGY STAR's comparison puts induction energy transfer at about 85 percent and conventional electric resistance at about 75 to 80 percent, with some manufacturers quoting induction near 90 percent. For the same pot of water, induction uses somewhat less electricity and gets there faster.
          </p>

          <p>
            The confusion comes from the numbers on the spec sheet. Induction zones are rated at high wattages, and many have boost settings that push even higher for short bursts. Those numbers describe how fast the cooktop can deliver energy, not how much it delivers over a meal. A zone that runs at high power for four minutes and then simmers at low power uses far less than its rating suggests.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Induction Electricity at a Glance</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~85-90%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Energy reaching the pan</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Cents</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical cost per meal</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">240V</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Built-in cooktop circuit</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1,800W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Portable 120V burner max</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you are switching from gas, your electricity use will go up, because you are moving cooking from the gas bill to the electric bill. But induction is much more efficient than gas at getting heat into the pan, so the total energy you buy for cooking drops. Whether the dollars go down too depends on local gas and electricity prices, which our <a href="/induction-vs-gas-cooktop/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">induction vs gas cooktop</a> comparison works through.
          </p>

          <p>
            Either way, cooking is a small part of most homes' energy use compared with heating, cooling, water heating and laundry. A switch to induction is not going to transform your bill in either direction. The bigger practical question is usually whether your electrical service can handle the cooktop, which we cover further down.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-vs-energy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Is the Difference Between Power Draw and Energy Used?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Power, measured in watts or kilowatts, is how fast an appliance uses electricity at a given moment. Energy, measured in kilowatt-hours, is how much it uses over time, and it is what your utility bills you for. A 2,000 watt zone running at full power for an hour uses 2 kilowatt-hours. The same zone running at full power for six minutes uses 0.2 kilowatt-hours.
          </p>

          <p>
            Induction cooktops have high power ratings because they are designed to heat quickly. Individual zones are commonly rated somewhere between about 1,200 and 3,700 watts depending on size, and boost modes can go higher for a few minutes. Add up all the zones on a 30 or 36 inch cooktop and the total rating can reach roughly 7 to 11 kilowatts. That total is a ceiling for sizing wiring, not a measure of what you will use.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Zone or Unit</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Rating</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What It Means</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Small zone</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1.2 to 1.8 kW</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Saucepans, sauces</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Large zone</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 2.0 to 3.7 kW</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stockpots, skillets</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Boost on a large zone</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Higher, for a few minutes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fast boiling</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Full cooktop total</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly 7 to 11 kW</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Used to size the circuit</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Portable burner</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Up to 1.8 kW</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Standard 120V outlet</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Most cooking happens at partial power. After a quick boil, simmering a pot needs only a fraction of a zone's maximum, and the hob delivers just that. Many cooktops also have power management that shares a fixed budget between zones, so turning on boost on one zone automatically limits another. You almost never run every zone at full power at once.
          </p>

          <p>
            The same logic applies to every appliance, which is why we break out running watts and real usage in guides like <a href="/how-much-electricity-does-a-dehumidifier-use/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how much electricity a dehumidifier uses</a> and <a href="/how-much-electricity-does-an-air-purifier-use/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how much electricity an air purifier uses</a>. The rated wattage tells you the worst case. Hours at each setting tell you the bill.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Much Does It Cost to Run an Induction Cooktop?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The math is simple: kilowatts multiplied by hours gives kilowatt-hours, and kilowatt-hours multiplied by your rate gives cost. Residential electricity prices vary widely by state and change over time, so check your own bill. For the examples here we use an illustrative rate of 17 cents per kilowatt-hour, which is in the general range of recent US residential averages. Substitute your own rate and the logic is identical.
          </p>

          <p>
            Running a 1.8 kilowatt zone at full power for an hour uses 1.8 kilowatt-hours, which at 17 cents costs about 31 cents. Running it at roughly half power for an hour costs about 15 cents. Those are the extremes for a single zone held for a full hour, which is longer than most cooking tasks take.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Example Task</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Approximate Energy</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost at 17¢/kWh</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Boil 2 liters of water</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 0.2 to 0.25 kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 4 cents</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Large zone at full power, 1 hour</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 2 to 3.7 kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 34 to 63 cents</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1.8 kW zone at full power, 1 hour</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.8 kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 31 cents</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1.8 kW zone at half power, 1 hour</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 0.9 kWh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 15 cents</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Boiling water makes a good check because the physics is fixed. Heating 2 liters of water from about room temperature to boiling takes roughly 0.19 kilowatt-hours of heat. At around 85 percent efficiency, induction uses about 0.22 kilowatt-hours to deliver that, or under 4 cents at our example rate. A radiant element at around 75 percent needs about 0.25 kilowatt-hours for the same pot.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a whole meal, add up the tasks. A weeknight pasta dinner with a boil, a simmer and a quick saute might use somewhere around half a kilowatt-hour on induction, which is less than 10 cents at our rate. Your numbers will differ with pan sizes, settings and how long you cook, but the scale is cents, not dollars.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="compare" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Does Induction Compare With Radiant Electric and Gas?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Against radiant electric, induction wins on energy for most tasks. The difference is a handful of percentage points in transfer efficiency, plus time saved because induction heats faster and does not need to warm an element and glass first. Radiant elements also keep releasing heat after you switch off, which you paid for but often do not use. Over a year that adds up to a modest saving, not a dramatic one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against gas, the efficiency gap is much larger. ENERGY STAR's figures put gas cooktops at roughly 32 percent energy transfer, because most of the flame's heat flows around the pan and into the room. Induction delivers more than twice as much of its input energy to the food. Whether that translates to a lower bill depends on what you pay for natural gas compared with electricity, which varies a lot by region.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Versus radiant electric: less electricity. Versus gas: more electricity, less total energy.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Switching from radiant to induction trims your electric use. Switching from gas moves cooking onto the electric bill, but with less energy wasted overall.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Our <a href="/induction-vs-electric-cooktop/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">induction vs electric cooktop</a> comparison covers the non-energy differences, such as control, cookware and cleaning, which are usually bigger reasons to choose one over the other than the energy bill.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more factor: standby power. Induction cooktops with electronic controls draw a small amount of power when off, as do most modern radiant cooktops with touch controls. It is a few watts at most and adds up to very little over a year, but it is not zero, so a cooktop is never completely free to own.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="circuit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Circuit Does an Induction Cooktop Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Full-size built-in induction cooktops and ranges in US homes typically need a dedicated 240 volt circuit. The breaker size depends on the model and is listed in the installation instructions; 40 and 50 amp circuits are common for 30 and 36 inch cooktops and ranges, while some smaller or power-limited models specify less. Always follow the manufacturer's specification, not a general rule.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are replacing a radiant electric cooktop or range, you may already have a suitable circuit. Check the existing breaker size and wire gauge against the new unit's requirements. If the new cooktop needs a larger breaker, the wire may need upgrading too, because the wire, not the breaker, sets the safe limit. That is a job for a licensed electrician.
          </p>

          <p>
            Portable induction burners are different. They plug into a standard 120 volt outlet and are commonly capped at around 1,800 watts, which is about what a typical 15 amp household circuit can supply. Running one on a circuit shared with a toaster, kettle or microwave can trip the breaker, so give it its own outlet on a kitchen circuit when you can.
          </p>

          <p>
            A newer category of induction ranges uses built-in batteries to plug into a standard 120 volt outlet, drawing modest power from the wall and topping up from the battery during heavy cooking. They cost more but can avoid new wiring entirely. Some conventional cooktops also offer installer-set power limits for homes with smaller circuits, at the cost of slower boost performance.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="panel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Will You Need a Panel Upgrade, and What Does It Cost?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most homes switching from radiant electric will not, because they already have the circuit. Homes switching from gas are the ones that sometimes need work. You need a free 240 volt circuit slot and enough capacity in the panel to carry the new load alongside everything else. Older homes with 100 amp service and lots of electric appliances are the most likely to be tight.
          </p>

          <p>
            An electrician can do a load calculation, which estimates your home's maximum demand using standard methods, and tell you whether the panel can take the cooktop. Often the answer is yes with a new circuit. Running a new 240 volt circuit to the kitchen is commonly a few hundred dollars to over a thousand, depending on distance, wall access and local rates.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the panel is full or undersized, the options are a panel upgrade, a smaller or power-limited cooktop, or a load-management device that prevents the cooktop and another big load from running at the same time. Panel upgrades are commonly quoted in a range of roughly $1,500 to $4,000, and more when the utility service line or meter also needs upgrading. Prices vary widely by region and change over time, so get local quotes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before paying for a panel upgrade, check for rebates. Some utilities and state programs have helped pay for electrical upgrades tied to induction cooktops or heat pumps, and federal programs have offered incentives at times. Availability changes, so ask your utility and your electrician what currently applies.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="save" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Can You Use Even Less Electricity Cooking on Induction?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Use a pan that matches the zone and sits flat. A magnetic base sized to the ring couples best with the coil, so more energy becomes heat in the pan. A small pan on a big zone or a warped base wastes energy and takes longer. Our <a href="/best-induction-cookware/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best induction cookware</a> guide focuses on pans that couple well.
          </p>

          <p>
            Put a lid on it. Covering a pot brings water to a boil faster and lets you simmer at a lower setting, on any cooktop. Use only as much water as you need for pasta or vegetables. Then drop to the lowest setting that holds a simmer, which on induction is reliable once you learn your hob.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use boost for what it is for: getting a large pot to a boil quickly. It does not save energy in itself, but it does not waste much either, because induction is efficient at every setting. What wastes energy is leaving zones running at high power longer than needed, so turn down as soon as you reach temperature.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your utility has time-of-use rates, cooking outside peak hours will cost less per kilowatt-hour, though cooking is rarely flexible enough for that to matter much. The bigger savings in most homes lie elsewhere, but cooking efficiently on induction is easy, and the cooktop rewards good habits faster than any other.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading the total rating as hourly use</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A cooktop rated at 10 kilowatts does not use 10 kilowatt-hours every hour you cook. That total is the most it could draw with every zone on full at once, which almost never happens. Real use depends on how many zones run, at what setting, for how long.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the existing circuit will do</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Induction cooktops specify a breaker size and wire gauge in the installation manual, and some need more than the radiant unit they replace. Check before buying, and have a licensed electrician confirm the circuit. Undersized wiring is a fire risk and can void the warranty.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sharing a portable burner's outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A portable induction burner can draw up to about 1,800 watts, close to the limit of a 15 amp circuit. Plugging it into the same circuit as a kettle, toaster or microwave can trip the breaker. Give it its own outlet and avoid light-duty extension cords.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the load calculation before switching from gas</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A home that cooked on gas may not have panel space or capacity for a 240 volt cooktop. An electrician's load calculation tells you whether a new circuit is enough or a panel upgrade is needed. Do it before you buy the cooktop, not on installation day.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a big drop in the bill</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Induction is efficient, but cooking is a small slice of most homes' energy use. Switching from radiant will trim the bill slightly; switching from gas moves cost from one bill to another. Choose induction for speed, control and cleaning, and treat any savings as a bonus.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many amps does an induction cooktop use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Built-in cooktops are usually installed on a dedicated 240 volt circuit, commonly 40 or 50 amps for 30 and 36 inch models, though some specify less. The breaker size sets the maximum; actual current depends on how many zones are on and at what setting. Check the installation manual for your exact model.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I plug an induction cooktop into a regular outlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Portable single or double burners plug into a standard 120 volt outlet and are commonly capped around 1,800 watts. Full-size built-in cooktops need a dedicated 240 volt circuit. A small number of battery-assisted induction ranges are designed for standard outlets, but conventional built-in cooktops are not.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is induction cheaper to run than gas?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Induction uses far less total energy than gas for the same cooking, but electricity usually costs more per unit of energy than natural gas. Whether induction is cheaper to run depends on your local rates. In many areas the difference is small either way, measured in a few dollars a month at most.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does an induction cooktop use electricity when it is off?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A small amount. Electronic controls and displays draw standby power, typically a few watts at most. Over a year that adds up to very little, similar to other appliances with touch controls and clocks. Turning off the breaker is not necessary to save energy and is not recommended for convenience.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does boost mode use a lot more electricity?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Boost draws more power for a short time, which heats the pan faster. Because it shortens the time needed, the total energy used to bring water to a boil is similar to a normal high setting. The waste comes from leaving a zone on high longer than needed, not from using boost.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much electricity does a portable induction burner use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At its maximum, a typical portable burner draws up to about 1.8 kilowatts, or 1.8 kilowatt-hours per hour at full power. Most cooking runs well below that. At an illustrative 17 cents per kilowatt-hour, an hour at full power costs about 31 cents and a typical task costs a few cents.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will an induction cooktop trip my breaker?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On a correctly sized dedicated circuit, it should not. Tripping usually means the circuit is undersized for the cooktop, it shares a circuit with other loads, or there is a fault. Portable units trip breakers most often when sharing a 15 amp circuit with other kitchen appliances. Persistent tripping warrants an electrician.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

