        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Induction does not use more electricity than other electric cooktops. It draws a lot of power for short bursts, but it puts more of that power into the pan and finishes faster, so a typical meal uses less and costs only a few cents. Compared with gas, it moves cooking onto the electric bill while cutting the total energy wasted.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The question worth spending time on is the circuit. Check your new cooktop's breaker requirement, confirm your existing wiring or get a load calculation if you are coming from gas, and look for rebates before paying for a panel upgrade. Get that right and induction is one of the cheapest, most efficient ways to cook.
          </p>
        </div>

